/* Pass 13 E: Iroh chapters 34-50, sharper homeworld continuity and tender dialogue. */
(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-34":{"number":34,"title":"Under the Same Lantern","content":"The lantern seller wanted to charge Iroh twice as much because he recognized him.\n\nI knew the moment it happened. The seller's shoulders went stiff, and his gaze moved from the old man's face to the fine embroidery at his collar.\n\n\"For the Dragon of the West,\" he said, \"this one would be appropriate.\"\n\nHe lifted a lantern with enormous painted flames. It was beautiful in the way an expensive decoration could be beautiful when someone else was paying for it. Iroh studied it with grave interest.\n\n\"I was considering the little turtleduck,\" he said.\n\nThe seller looked baffled. The turtleduck lantern was crooked, inexpensive, and painted with one eye slightly larger than the other.\n\nI loved it immediately.\n\n\"That one,\" I said.\n\n\"Of course,\" the seller replied, recovering. \"For your companion.\"\n\nThe word made me hesitate. It wasn't wrong, but the market was crowded. People knew Iroh; some of them also knew enough about his history to be curious. I had no intention of turning an evening's walk into a public explanation of who loved whom.\n\nIroh noticed my pause.\n\n\"Would you prefer we look elsewhere?\" he asked.\n\n\"No. I want the turtleduck.\"\n\n\"Then we shall have the turtleduck.\"\n\nHe paid the marked price and accepted change, despite the seller's attempt to refuse it. I carried the lantern while he carried a paper bag of steamed buns. We had promised Mina we would not return to the shop with a pile of unbudgeted decorations, so we were already operating at the very edge of our allowance.\n\n\"She will be merciless,\" I said.\n\n\"She has that right.\"\n\n\"She'll say you bought an animal that can't swim.\"\n\n\"It is a lantern. Its swimming abilities are not relevant.\"\n\nThe market curved along the river, where strings of smaller lights reflected in black water. Children ran between stalls. A pair of musicians played an uneven tune near the bridge. A woman selling ribbons called out that she had one shade of gold left before closing.\n\nIroh offered me his arm.\n\nI wanted it. I also wanted the option to let go if the crowd became too much.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said, slipping my hand around his elbow. \"But if someone starts asking personal questions, I get to stop the conversation.\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\n\"And you don't have to introduce me as anything you're uncomfortable saying.\"\n\nHe stopped walking.\n\n\"I am not ashamed of you.\"\n\n\"I didn't say you were.\"\n\n\"No.\" He considered. \"You are asking me not to turn my pride into an announcement you did not choose.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nA child nearly collided with my knees while chasing a loose lantern. Iroh caught the lantern by its handle, handed it back, and asked the child to walk near the water. He didn't offer a speech about rivers. The child ran off anyway. We shared a look.\n\n\"An excellent success,\" he said.\n\n\"Your authority is terrifying.\"\n\nWe passed a familiar voice. Ren stood at a stall buying stationery, her arms full of papers. She greeted Iroh with a discreet nod, then looked at me.\n\n\"Enjoying the festival?\"\n\n\"I am,\" I said. \"Especially because he hasn't tried to buy the whole street.\"\n\n\"Give him time.\"\n\nIroh sighed. \"I am surrounded by critics.\"\n\nRen left with a smile. She didn't ask about our plans or the relationships I'd spoken about privately with Iroh. Not every person who knew him needed to know everything about us.\n\nA small group had gathered beside the musicians, debating whether to hang a row of lanterns across the narrow market passage. The seller who owned the next stall objected; the string would catch the awning every time he lowered it.\n\nIroh looked at the empty space above the crowd.\n\n\"Perhaps if we tie it higher,\" he began.\n\n\"Then the ladder will block the street,\" I said.\n\nThe seller nodded, grateful someone had noticed.\n\nWe walked around the stalls until we found a wider arch where the musicians could still see the display. The lantern maker offered to lend a safer string, but only if somebody agreed to collect it before the market closed. Ren took the evening shift after checking her own plans.\n\n\"An evening of extraordinary negotiations,\" Iroh murmured.\n\n\"All so a turtleduck can glow.\"\n\nHe lifted the crooked lantern. \"A very noble turtleduck.\"\n\nThe children passing beneath the arch laughed at its uneven eye. One asked whether she could carry it in the procession. Iroh looked at me.\n\n\"Does she want to?\" I asked her.\n\n\"Only to the bridge.\"\n\n\"Then ask the lantern keeper whether it's allowed.\"\n\nIt was. The child carried the lantern a short distance, handed it back carefully, and ran to catch up with her family. We walked behind her, with our hands almost touching.\n\nIroh glanced at me. \"Are you worried about being seen together?\"\n\n\"Not with you. I'm worried about strangers deciding what being seen means they can ask.\"\n\n\"Then we need not answer.\"\n\nI smiled. Our relationship had room for public tenderness and private choices. I'd told him openly that I preferred polyamory and was ethically non-monogamous, and he hadn't treated that truth as a riddle for the market to solve.\n\nWhen the slower music began, he offered his hand again. I took it. There were no speeches, only his ridiculous lantern, a tune we could barely follow, and the faint warmth of his fingers around mine.\n\nFarther along, the musicians started a slower song. Iroh offered his hand. \"Would you dance?\"\n\n\"Here?\"\n\n\"Unless you have another river in mind.\"\n\n\"I'm terrible.\"\n\n\"Then we will be terrible together.\"\n\nWe danced beside the railing. It was more of a careful shuffle than anything that could be called elegant, and I laughed when he misjudged a step and nearly turned the turtleduck upside down. He caught my hand before I could trip. I caught his sleeve. We steadied each other, still laughing.\n\nFor a moment I forgot we were being watched at all. Then I noticed a couple smiling from a nearby bench. I felt the old instinct to explain myself, to describe every other relationship so nobody would assume this was the only person in my life.\n\nI didn't owe them that. Neither did Iroh.\n\n\"I like being here with you,\" I said.\n\nHe rested his forehead briefly against mine. \"And I with you.\"\n\n\"What if people misunderstand?\"\n\n\"Then we can correct what matters, and let the rest pass.\"\n\n\"That sounds dangerously reasonable.\"\n\n\"I am occasionally afflicted.\"\n\nHe asked before kissing me. I answered by taking one small step closer. The kiss tasted of sweet bun and cold evening air. A nearby musician missed a note; Iroh laughed against my cheek, and that made me laugh too.\n\nWe walked home beneath the lanterns without pretending our public affection had resolved every private agreement. I was still ethically non-monogamous, still happiest with polyamory, and still free to make plans with other people who knew and chose that arrangement. Iroh knew, and he had the same freedom to name his own needs.\n\nAt the shop, Mina saw the turtleduck and put both hands on her hips.\n\n\"How much?\"\n\n\"The price on the tag,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked proud of himself.\n\n\"Good,\" Mina said. \"Then tomorrow you can help me fix its crooked eye.\"\n\nWe hung it above the long table, where it cast a small, uneven pool of light. Later he invited me upstairs. I said yes, and we left the door closed on the part of the evening that didn't belong to the market.\n\nBy morning the turtleduck had become Mina's favorite lantern. She would never admit it, but she moved it away from the stove so the heat couldn't scorch the paint. I saw Iroh notice. Neither of us said a word.","word_count":1285},"avatar-35":{"number":35,"title":"The General and the Gardener","content":"The basil looked dreadful.\n\nIroh stood over the pot as though it had violated the terms of a peace treaty.\n\n\"It has refused every reasonable offer,\" he said.\n\n\"It needs water.\"\n\n\"That is one interpretation.\"\n\nI crouched beside the pot and touched the soil. Dry as dust. The leaves at the bottom had curled in on themselves, and a thin crack ran along the earth beside the stem.\n\nThe courtyard behind the Jasmine Dragon wasn't grand. It held a row of herbs for the kitchen, a stubborn vine I had once labeled NOT TEA, and a few flowers planted because bees visited them. It smelled of warm brick and soil; a place where people could be ordinary even after doing extraordinary things.\n\nIroh brought a watering can and raised it.\n\n\"Not all at once,\" I said.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Too much water runs past the roots. Give it time.\"\n\nHe poured a little. We waited for it to soak in.\n\nLian, the neighbor on the other side of the wall, leaned over with a basket in her arms.\n\n\"Your mint is blocking my delivery path again.\"\n\nIroh looked at the stems.\n\n\"An ambitious plant.\"\n\n\"It's a nuisance,\" she answered, though she was smiling. \"Can you trim it today?\"\n\n\"We will,\" I said.\n\nAfter she left, Iroh reached for the shears.\n\n\"Shall we launch the campaign?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Let's measure how much of the path needs clearing first.\"\n\nHe paused.\n\n\"Very well. A campaign with boundaries.\"\n\nWe measured the path. The stems had grown farther than I'd realized, and removing them without checking might have left the roots exposed to the afternoon sun. We cut only the overhang and carried the trimmings to the kitchen to dry.\n\nMina accepted the mint with a suspicious look.\n\n\"Did you wash it?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" I admitted.\n\n\"Then it isn't ready for tea.\"\n\nIroh bowed.\n\n\"Our victory has been postponed.\"\n\nShe rolled her eyes and handed us a clean bowl.\n\nWhen we returned to the courtyard, I found him staring at the empty section of path.\n\n\"You're thinking about something else,\" I said.\n\n\"Am I?\"\n\n\"Possibly about how the garden has failed to acknowledge your leadership.\"\n\nHe laughed, but the sound faded.\n\n\"I used to think every problem could be arranged into a battle. Even when I wanted to grow something.\"\n\n\"Do you still?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. There were years when I was praised for conquering things. I still reach for those words before I find better ones.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Do the plants mind?\"\n\n\"No. But I do.\"\n\nHe looked toward the empty chair under the tree.\n\n\"Lu Ten liked gardens,\" he said. \"He liked the way they kept changing after the work was done. He would put his chair somewhere new every day to see what he had missed.\"\n\nI didn't answer immediately. The memory belonged to him; I did not want to make it into a lesson about how much happier he ought to be now.\n\n\"Would you like the chair moved?\" I asked.\n\n\"Perhaps tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow.\"\n\nIroh took the trowel and turned it over in his hands.\n\n\"Will you show me how to repot the basil? Without pretending I know the answer?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nWe used a pot with drainage holes and fresh soil. He loosened the old roots carefully, following my instructions with more patience than he'd shown his tea kettle the day before. I held the stem steady while he settled the new soil around it.\n\nWhen he finished, he looked expectantly at the leaves.\n\nNothing happened.\n\n\"Is it cured?\" he asked.\n\n\"Plants aren't obliged to perform gratitude.\"\n\n\"Tragic.\"\n\nMina returned with a small ledger she kept for the garden. It listed which herbs the kitchen actually used, how much water the courtyard needed in summer, and which beds belonged to the neighbor.\n\n\"The basil isn't a guest who can survive on compliments,\" she said.\n\nIroh looked stricken. \"I have been most encouraging.\"\n\n\"Try a watering schedule.\"\n\nWe made one, with room to record whether the soil was still damp from the day before. Lian looked over the wall to say the watering can was dripping onto her bricks. Iroh lifted it, examined the loose spout, and asked whether she wanted him to fix it before making another puddle.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"And no more mystery vines.\"\n\nI laughed. The vine labeled NOT TEA had become a minor legend, but it was also a reminder that we couldn't use plants simply because they smelled interesting. We called for a gardener to identify anything new before it went into a cup.\n\nIroh sat on the low stone bench when the work was done. He looked tired, but it wasn't a sad tiredness.\n\n\"I was once praised for taking cities,\" he said. \"Today I have learned to water one small pot.\"\n\n\"Which are you more proud of?\"\n\nHe looked at the curled leaves. \"I think I know which one I want to remember.\"\n\nI took his hand. I wanted to tell him he had become good, that everything harmful in his past had been repaired by growing basil. That would have been untrue. The work he'd done here mattered without paying for absolution from people he'd never met.\n\n\"May I sit with you?\" I asked.\n\n\"You already are.\"\n\n\"Then may I stay after supper?\"\n\nHis smile was almost shy. \"If you wish.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nThe shop would reopen tomorrow. The basil would need checking. We had other people, tasks and affections in our lives, and nothing about this little garden made those choices disappear. I leaned against his shoulder until the afternoon light moved off the wall.\n\nWe made a watering schedule on a scrap of paper. Mina had told us she couldn't leave the breakfast rush to inspect the pots, so we put the check after lunch. Iroh asked whether I wanted to do it every morning.\n\n\"Some mornings,\" I said. \"Not all of them. I have other places to be, and sometimes the Threadway schedule changes.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe had talked about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory; the garden wasn't a symbol of exclusivity or ownership. He could care for it when I was away. I could return to find it changed. If either of us needed help, we could ask.\n\n\"Then I will keep the record when you're gone,\" he said.\n\n\"And let me see it when I come back.\"\n\n\"Without marking your answers wrong?\"\n\n\"Let's not make promises we can't keep.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nLian returned with a small jar of cuttings she no longer had room to grow. The roots were white and strong.\n\n\"Do you want these?\" she asked. \"I can't put them near my drain. They spread too quickly.\"\n\nIroh looked tempted.\n\n\"They would look splendid along the wall.\"\n\n\"They would look splendid everywhere,\" Lian said. \"That's the problem.\"\n\nWe examined the jar together. I had been thinking only about making our patch of green look fuller. I hadn't considered what would happen to the neighboring beds after a season of unchecked growth.\n\n\"What if we keep one in a separate pot?\" I asked.\n\nLian nodded. \"That could work if you cut off the seed heads and check it every week.\"\n\nIroh considered the extra work.\n\n\"Perhaps we have enough to care for already.\"\n\nHe thanked her and declined the cuttings.\n\nIt surprised me how much I liked that choice. A garden wasn't made better simply by collecting every beautiful thing that crossed its boundary.\n\nWhen Lian had gone, I wrote a little note beside the watering schedule: *Before adding a plant, ask who will care for it.*\n\nIroh read it.\n\n\"Before offering advice,\" he said, \"perhaps one should also ask who will live with it.\"\n\n\"Are you talking about gardening?\"\n\n\"At this moment, yes.\"\n\nHe looked far too pleased with himself.\n\nAt dusk we sat beneath the climbing vine with two cups of tea. Iroh kept glancing at the basil.\n\n\"It still looks terrible,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then I suppose we have done what we can.\"\n\n\"For today.\"\n\nHe held out his hand.\n\n\"Would you like company?\"\n\n\"I would.\"\n\nI moved close enough that our shoulders touched. He smelled of tea and fresh soil, and I caught a little dirt on his sleeve.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Even in my present disreputable condition?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nThe kiss was warm, unhurried and a little awkward because his tea cup was still between us. We laughed, set it aside, and kissed again.\n\nLater we went upstairs and closed the door. The garden needed nothing more from us that night.\n\nThree mornings later, the basil sent out one new green leaf. Iroh came to find me before breakfast, delight bright in his eyes.\n\n\"Victory!\" he announced.\n\nI raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe looked toward the plant, then back at me.\n\n\"A promising development,\" he amended.\n\nI kissed his cheek.\n\nWe made tea and allowed the garden to grow at its own pace.","word_count":1492},"avatar-44":{"number":44,"title":"Rain in the Second Kitchen","content":"The roof began leaking just as Iroh declared that the evening would be peaceful.\n\nA drop of water landed in his tea.\n\nHe stared at the surface.\n\nAnother drop followed.\n\n\"Ah,\" he said.\n\nI looked up.\n\nThe ceiling above the front counter had developed a dark seam. Rainwater ran along it, gathered at the lowest point, and fell directly into the pot of jasmine leaves.\n\n\"Please tell me that's a new blend,\" I said.\n\n\"Only if we intend to serve roof.\"\n\nBy the time we moved the tea, three customers were holding cups in their laps to protect them from the ceiling. Iroh apologized, offered fresh drinks from the back, and asked them to use the side entrance until the leak could be inspected.\n\nMara appeared at the door with a basket of bread and took in the scene.\n\n\"Oh, lovely,\" she said. \"Indoor weather.\"\n\n\"An exciting new service,\" Iroh replied.\n\n\"Charge extra.\"\n\nWe moved the tables away from the wet floor. The shop wasn't flooded, but the water had found a narrow channel down the wall, and the front room smelled of damp plaster. The roof had been patched after the last storm. Apparently someone had missed a seam.\n\nTamsin arrived with a ladder and an expression that suggested she was tired of buildings having opinions.\n\n\"I can't climb onto wet slate,\" she told us. \"Not safely. I'll seal the interior drip and inspect the roof when it dries.\"\n\nIroh nodded.\n\n\"Then we shall close early.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nShe looked surprised by how quickly he agreed.\n\nHe noticed.\n\n\"I have learned that hospitality does not require guests to swim.\"\n\nThat left the two of us, a mountain of cups, and the second kitchen.\n\nIt was barely large enough for a person to turn around without becoming intimately acquainted with a shelf. The old stove sat against one wall. A narrow table occupied the other, and a basket of spare cloths lived beneath it.\n\nI carried in a kettle. Iroh carried the tea tins.\n\nWe collided in the doorway.\n\n\"After you,\" he said.\n\n\"There's nowhere for me to go.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps we should invent a new direction.\"\n\nI stepped sideways. He stepped the same way. The kettle knocked against the door frame.\n\nWe both laughed.\n\nThe back kitchen had always been our quiet place, but quiet looked different when two people were trying to run a business from it. The shelf smelled of ginger and dried citrus. A tiny window faced the garden, where the rain had turned the path into a series of bright dark stones.\n\nI put the dirty cups in the washbasin.\n\nIroh reached for the clean ones.\n\nWe bumped elbows.\n\n\"You're doing that on purpose,\" I said.\n\n\"Would it improve your opinion of me if I were?\"\n\n\"Probably not.\"\n\n\"Then I shall maintain my innocence.\"\n\nBy the third collision, I was warm enough to suspect he had turned the kitchen into a game. He was smiling too much for a man whose roof had leaked into his tea.\n\nWe worked until the last cup was clean. Then we took inventory. The damaged tea had to be discarded. Two tins of the expensive blend had survived. One crate of cups would need washing again when the dust settled.\n\nThe numbers added up to a loss we couldn't ignore.\n\nIroh studied the page.\n\n\"We can afford the roof repair,\" he said. \"But the next order of mountain mint will be smaller.\"\n\nI leaned against the counter.\n\n\"You hate reducing the good tea.\"\n\n\"I hate choosing which good thing to give up.\"\n\n\"Then we choose carefully.\"\n\nHe looked at me, tired but amused.\n\n\"You sound like Neri.\"\n\n\"That's insulting.\"\n\n\"Only a little.\"\n\nWe made a temporary menu for the back kitchen, four drinks instead of twelve. A sign at the front door explained the closure, the rain damage, and when customers could expect an update. No promise of tomorrow. No claim that the weather owed us cooperation.\n\nBy evening the storm had begun to ease.\n\nMina arrived with a damp inventory sheet and a sharper expression than the weather deserved.\n\n\"The roof isn't the only problem,\" she said. \"Two of the clean tea tins were under the leak. They're closed, but I don't know whether the water got inside.\"\n\nIroh reached for one. Mina stopped him.\n\n\"We don't use it until we've checked it properly. I can mark it as held stock.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" he said.\n\nShe handed him the chalk. He wrote HOLD — INSPECTION beside the tins, then looked almost offended that such a small word could keep him from serving tea.\n\nWe moved the containers to a dry shelf and recorded how much stock was still safe. The repair estimate would have to include the damaged plaster and the work needed to check for lingering dampness, not just a patch where the water had fallen. Tamsin had already told us that a dry afternoon wasn't proof the structure could take another storm.\n\n\"Will the customers be disappointed?\" I asked.\n\n\"Certainly,\" Iroh said. \"They may choose the smaller menu, or return when we can offer more.\"\n\nHe sounded sad. I touched his sleeve.\n\n\"You've built a place people like,\" I said. \"They can keep liking it while a room is closed.\"\n\nHe looked toward the front counter. \"I wonder why I find that difficult to believe.\"\n\n\"Because you're terrible at letting anything unfinished stay unfinished.\"\n\n\"An accusation made with troubling confidence.\"\n\nI smiled. Mina had gone home at her agreed hour. We were alone with the rain, the little candle and a kitchen that smelled of wet plaster.\n\n\"Do you want to sit with me for a while?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\nHe took my hand before we went to the table. There was no need to make the leak romantic, and no reason affection had to wait until every repair was finished.\n\nIroh lit one candle on the narrow table. I stood beside the stove, rolling my shoulders to loosen the ache from moving furniture.\n\n\"Come here,\" he said.\n\n\"What for?\"\n\n\"Your shoulders.\"\n\n\"I can still move them.\"\n\n\"An achievement. Come here anyway.\"\n\nI went.\n\nHe placed his hands on either side of my neck and began working his thumbs into the tense muscles beneath my collar. He was careful, pausing whenever I shifted. The warmth of his hands moved slowly through the ache.\n\n\"Better?\" he asked.\n\n\"Much.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps you will stop trying to carry the whole teahouse by yourself.\"\n\n\"That sounds suspiciously like advice you should take.\"\n\n\"Many of my finest teachings are things I have difficulty practicing.\"\n\nI turned toward him. Our faces were close. The candlelight softened the lines beside his eyes, and the faint smell of jasmine clung to his sleeve.\n\n\"You don't have to pretend the leak doesn't bother you,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Does it?\"\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe laughed quietly.\n\n\"I am fond of this shop,\" he admitted. \"I built parts of it with my hands. I like knowing that people can come here and feel safe, even for the length of a cup of tea. When the roof leaks, it feels like I have failed to protect that promise.\"\n\n\"A roof is allowed to fail without making its owner a failure.\"\n\n\"An irritatingly sensible observation.\"\n\n\"Learned from somebody.\"\n\nHe rested his forehead lightly against mine.\n\n\"Who could that be?\"\n\n\"I believe he has a very fine beard.\"\n\n\"An excellent teacher, then.\"\n\nI kissed him. He answered with the patient warmth I knew so well, then drew me closer when I slipped my hands around his waist. The little kitchen felt suddenly smaller, though I didn't mind.\n\nHe smiled against my mouth.\n\n\"The kettle is behind you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And the table is behind me.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"We have very little room.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps,\" I whispered, \"we should stop trying to move.\"\n\nHe laughed, soft and delighted. We stood together until the candle burned low and the rain became a whisper against the window.\n\nWhen we finally locked the shop, he asked whether I wanted to stay the night.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said.\n\nWe went upstairs, leaving the clean cups drying in the second kitchen. The rest of the evening needed no audience.\n\nIn the morning, the ceiling still had a stain. Tamsin returned with safe weather and a repair estimate. We signed the work order, posted the limited menu, and began again.\n\nIroh poured the first fresh cup for himself.\n\nHe examined it carefully.\n\n\"No roof,\" I said.\n\n\"A pity. I had considered patenting the blend.\"\n\nI laughed, and he handed the second cup to me.","word_count":1428},"avatar-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Price of the Mountain Mint","content":"The supplier's letter smelled of rain and spoiled leaves.\n\nIroh read it at the back counter, his expression growing more serious with every line.\n\n\"Bad news?\" I asked.\n\n\"The mountain harvest was poor. Too much rain at the wrong time.\"\n\nHe handed me the note.\n\nThe supplier had lost nearly half the expected mint crop. Transporting what remained would cost more, and the farmers had asked their regular buyers to take smaller orders rather than forcing prices low enough to leave families without income.\n\n\"They're trying to share the loss,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Can we manage with less?\"\n\nIroh looked toward the tea tins.\n\n\"We can. But not if I insist on serving everything as though nothing has changed.\"\n\nHe sounded unhappy about it.\n\nThe Jasmine Dragon had already lost stock in the roof leak. We needed another shipment, but the price was now nearly twice what we'd planned. The bakery was feeling the same shortage in some of its herbs. At the clinic, Katara's volunteers had been rationing mint for certain medicinal preparations.\n\nNone of this could be solved by wishing a more generous harvest into existence.\n\nWe called a meeting with the people who relied on the shipments.\n\nMara arrived with flour on her sleeve. Tamsin brought a pencil and the expression of somebody prepared to object to numbers that didn't add up. Neri came with the route schedule. Aang had sent a note from Riverstone asking us to prioritize the clinic's confirmed medicinal needs.\n\nIroh poured a simpler tea than usual.\n\nNobody complained.\n\n\"We can divide what remains,\" he said, \"but I will not ask the growers to sell at a loss.\"\n\nMara nodded.\n\n\"Good. They're people, not a cupboard.\"\n\nThe figures were less encouraging.\n\nThe clinic needed a guaranteed supply. The bakery could change two recipes. The teahouse could reduce its premium blend, but enough customers depended on the ordinary tea for a small comfort during their working day that raising every price would hurt people who had little to spare.\n\n\"Then don't raise every price,\" I said.\n\nTamsin lifted an eyebrow.\n\n\"Where does the money come from?\"\n\n\"Let's find out before we guess.\"\n\nWe listed the costs on one sheet: tea, fuel, clean water, staff time, transport, cup repairs, and an emergency reserve. Iroh watched me write.\n\n\"You're forgetting something,\" he said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Payment for the growers' risk.\"\n\nI added it.\n\nThe numbers became uglier.\n\nMara smiled.\n\n\"At least they're honest.\"\n\nA good part of the morning went into arguing over what fair meant. Iroh wanted everyone to have the best tea he could serve. Tamsin wanted the business to survive. Neri wanted the delivery route kept open. I wanted us to stop talking as though these desires were enemies.\n\nIn the end, we designed three offerings.\n\nThe ordinary house blend would remain affordable. It used local herbs and roasted grain with a little citrus peel, warm and pleasant without pretending to be rare mountain mint. A smaller premium service would cost more and use the limited mint supply, with the price clearly explained. The clinic's confirmed allotment would be reserved before any tea was sold.\n\n\"No free miracle,\" I said.\n\n\"No,\" Iroh agreed. \"Just careful work.\"\n\nWe posted the menu that afternoon.\n\nThe first customer read it, frowned, and asked why the famous mountain mint tea had become so expensive.\n\nIroh explained the harvest without turning the farmers into a tragedy for entertainment.\n\n\"Then what is that one?\" the customer asked, pointing to the house blend.\n\n\"A new recipe,\" Iroh said. \"It may disappoint you.\"\n\nThe customer sniffed the cup.\n\n\"Smells good.\"\n\n\"It is intended to.\"\n\nThey tasted it.\n\n\"Hm.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"I'll have another.\"\n\nIroh's eyebrows rose.\n\nWe both tried not to look relieved.\n\nThe second customer complained that a famous shop shouldn't serve a cheaper alternative. The third asked if the house blend came in a travel flask. By evening, we'd sold more of it than we'd expected.\n\nNot everyone was pleased. That was normal. A business could make a fair decision and still disappoint someone.\n\nMina brought the day's receipts over and helped us compare the cost of the new house blend with the old mountain mint. She'd been fielding customer complaints since morning and had a better idea than either of us about which substitutions people actually liked.\n\n\"We should write the reason for the change on the menu,\" she said. \"Not a sad story about the mountain. Just the supply shortage and the new price.\"\n\nIroh nodded. \"And the growers should be paid what they asked.\"\n\n\"Yes. That should be in the ledger, not in the speech you plan to make.\"\n\nHe looked wounded. I laughed into my sleeve.\n\nWe calculated what it would cost to keep one small tin of the rarer mint for customers who specifically wanted it. The rest of the menu could use local herbs whose sources had been checked. No one suggested sneaking unverified plants into a blend to make it seem luxurious.\n\nIroh wrote the numbers neatly, then looked up at me.\n\n\"Do you mind that our next evening won't be quite so extravagant?\"\n\n\"What did you plan?\"\n\n\"Tea. A walk. Possibly two sweet buns.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible disappointment.\"\n\nHis smile brightened. \"Two very good sweet buns.\"\n\nI reached across the counter to touch his hand. We had spoken openly about how I preferred polyamory and practiced ethical non-monogamy. A chosen evening together didn't become less intimate because I could love other people, or because the expensive mint had become scarce.\n\n\"Then I would like your company more than the rare tea,\" I said.\n\n\"An excellent investment.\"\n\nHe leaned close and waited. I kissed him briefly, then we returned to the till before Mina could accuse us of holding up the accounts. There were growers to pay and tomorrow's menu to prepare. This particular romance came with receipts.\n\nAfter closing, I helped Iroh count the till. He checked each coin twice, separated payment owed to workers from money needed for stock, and put a small sum in the repair reserve.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"I was remembering an old habit.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"Thinking generosity meant giving something beautiful without asking what it cost.\"\n\nI leaned against the counter.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think generosity sometimes means saying the price aloud and making sure the people who grow the tea can eat.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like a lesson worth keeping.\"\n\nHe closed the ledger.\n\n\"Would you like to taste the final blend? I changed the citrus balance.\"\n\n\"Is that an invitation or a request for a quality-control volunteer?\"\n\n\"Both. I am very efficient.\"\n\nWe brewed the last test cup together.\n\nIt tasted of toasted grain, orange peel, and a touch of wild rosemary. It was not the expensive tea. It was good on its own terms.\n\n\"I like it,\" I said.\n\n\"You needn't be kind.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThen he pulled a small paper packet from his sleeve.\n\n\"One thing the supplier included.\"\n\nInside was a handful of dried mountain mint, no more than enough for a private pot.\n\n\"They sent a gift?\"\n\n\"A thank-you for accepting the reduced order.\"\n\nI touched the edge of the packet.\n\n\"Are you going to save it for someone important?\"\n\n\"I had thought of sharing it with someone important.\"\n\nI met his eyes.\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it, then looked suddenly shy in a way that still surprised me after all our time together.\n\n\"Tomorrow evening?\" he asked.\n\n\"I would love that.\"\n\nHe tucked the packet away again. We walked home past the lanterns, with the new menu posted and the accounts balanced as far as they could be balanced.\n\nThe next day, a farmer from the supplier's district sent a brief note thanking the teahouse for paying the agreed price. It wasn't a blessing from the spirits or a sudden end to the shortage.\n\nIt was proof that someone on the other side of the road had been treated fairly.\n\nIroh placed the note beside the ledger.\n\nWhen the kettle boiled that evening, he opened the small packet.\n\nWe shared the mint without pretending it had cost nothing.","word_count":1358},"avatar-46":{"number":46,"title":"A Game Neither of Us Wins","content":"I won the first game of Pai Sho because Iroh wasn't paying attention.\n\nThat should have been impossible.\n\nHe'd taught me the opening himself, explaining the balance between movement and patience with the kind of delight most people reserved for a very good dessert. I was improving, but only in the way a determined student improves when the teacher is much better and very kind.\n\nYet here he was, staring at a tile instead of the board.\n\n\"I think that's the end,\" I said.\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\nI pointed.\n\nHe studied the position for several seconds, then set down the tile in his hand.\n\n\"So it is.\"\n\nI sat back.\n\n\"You've never surrendered that quickly.\"\n\n\"I am capable of losing.\"\n\n\"Yes, but you usually lose while delivering a lecture about why losing is good for the soul.\"\n\nHe smiled, though it didn't quite reach his eyes.\n\nThe teahouse was quiet. A light rain tapped the repaired roof. The new affordable house blend had sold well enough to cover the week's deliveries, and a small packet of mountain mint sat on the shelf, carefully saved for special occasions. There was no emergency that required either of us to stay awake.\n\nWhich left the problem Iroh wasn't saying aloud.\n\n\"Are you worried about something?\" I asked.\n\nHe folded his hands.\n\n\"Only the ordinary things.\"\n\n\"That is not an answer.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps you have learned another lesson.\"\n\nI waited.\n\nHe looked at the board.\n\n\"Earlier, a visitor asked whether you and Maui would be attending the next Lantern Walk together.\"\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\n\"They seemed pleased by the thought.\"\n\n\"People like watching him make a fool of himself with decorations.\"\n\n\"Yes. He is very enthusiastic.\"\n\nIroh smiled, then let it fade.\n\n\"I found myself wondering whether there would still be room for tea that evening.\"\n\nThere it was.\n\nI didn't laugh. The vulnerability in his voice didn't deserve a joke, even though I wanted to soften it.\n\n\"Would you like to make plans with me?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I would. But I did not wish to sound as though I were asking you to choose.\"\n\n\"You can ask for time without asking me to stop caring about other people.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"We have discussed ethical non-monogamy and polyamory before,\" he said. \"I understand that love need not be a contest. Still, I find that understanding does not prevent a foolish thought from visiting.\"\n\n\"You're not foolish.\"\n\n\"I am sometimes.\"\n\n\"All right. You can be foolish. But you don't have to hide it.\"\n\nHe looked at me, and some of the tension eased.\n\nI moved the Pai Sho board aside.\n\n\"Tell me what you're afraid of.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"That I will become the pleasant place you visit when you need rest, and then leave behind when you are ready for excitement.\"\n\nI felt the words settle between us.\n\n\"I love this place,\" I said. \"But I don't love you because you're restful. I love you because you tell me when I'm being impatient. Because you remember the names of people the square forgets. Because you have a terrible sense of humor and an even worse habit of making me tea when I haven't asked.\"\n\nHe chuckled.\n\n\"And because you're willing to tell me when you're frightened,\" I added. \"Even when it's difficult.\"\n\n\"I have not been particularly brave tonight.\"\n\n\"You have.\"\n\nWe sat in silence for a while. The rain moved across the roof in uneven rhythms. Somewhere beyond the kitchen, the repaired gutter clicked as water passed through it.\n\n\"Would it help to plan our next evening?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then let's look at the calendar.\"\n\nWe did.\n\nI had promised to help Greg with the listening fair at Starwake. Maui wanted assistance checking the sea-glass orchard after a storm. Katara was still away in the South, and I had agreed to help organize supplies for the Riverstone clinic volunteers. None of those commitments were romantic emergencies. They were part of the life I had chosen.\n\nThe calendar had a mark beside the Listening Fair that Iroh didn't recognize.\n\n\"A small circle,\" he said. \"Does that mean you have promised to perform?\"\n\n\"No. It means Greg invited me to help. I haven't agreed to a shift.\"\n\nHe looked relieved. \"Then you can still decide.\"\n\n\"Yes. And so can he.\"\n\nWe took out a separate page for the promises I'd actually made. The difference between an invitation and a commitment had been a hard lesson for everyone in the vale. It was particularly hard for Iroh, who'd spent years interpreting a request for help as something he should fulfill before anybody could ask twice.\n\n\"Can I be selfish for a moment?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\n\"I would like one evening where you come to the shop without having to explain what comes next. I know you may visit other people. I only want to enjoy this moment without planning the next parting.\"\n\nThe wish surprised me. Iroh so often answered with warm patience that I'd begun to forget he could be afraid of running out of time.\n\n\"I can do that tonight,\" I said. \"Tomorrow I have plans I intend to keep.\"\n\n\"Then tonight.\"\n\nHis shoulders eased. He touched the Pai Sho tile we'd left at the edge of the board.\n\n\"Would you like a rematch?\"\n\n\"Not if you're going to let me win.\"\n\n\"An outrageous suggestion. I intend to defeat you magnificently.\"\n\nI laughed and moved my first piece. For a while we played badly on purpose, making up ridiculous commentary for each tile. The outside world still held people I loved and work I wanted to do. Here was a man who wanted to tease me over a board game, not ask me to prove that he was the most important road in my life.\n\nWhen the kettle began to hiss, he looked up at me with an expression so fond I forgot my strategy. He took the opportunity to win a piece.\n\n\"Cheater,\" I accused.\n\n\"An old general must take the opportunities available.\"\n\nI was still laughing when we put the board away.\n\nWe found an evening for tea, then another for a walk through Jasmine Steps.\n\nIroh looked at the page.\n\n\"That is a rather large number of people in your life.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"And yet you are here.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nHe reached across the table for my hand.\n\n\"Then perhaps I should stop making a question of what is already an answer.\"\n\n\"Not stop asking questions,\" I said. \"Just ask me the real ones.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI picked up the Pai Sho tile I'd won with.\n\n\"One more game?\"\n\nHe looked at the board.\n\n\"You are taking advantage of an elderly man in emotional distress.\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"Shameless.\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe reset the tiles.\n\nThis time he paid attention.\n\nI lost in twelve moves.\n\n\"That's more like it,\" I said.\n\n\"I hope the defeat was instructive.\"\n\n\"Deeply.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nAfter the game, we carried the cups into the small second kitchen. The roof no longer leaked, but the room still felt intimate in a way the public teahouse did not. He stood close enough that I could smell the citrus in his sleeve.\n\n\"May I offer you something other than tea?\" he asked.\n\n\"That depends.\"\n\n\"On what?\"\n\n\"Whether the offer involves another strategic defeat.\"\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"I had hoped for a kiss.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That seems fair.\"\n\nHis hand rested at my waist, warm and steady. I kissed him slowly, then again when he murmured something about me having very poor negotiating skills. The kettle began to hiss. We ignored it for just long enough to make the joke inevitable.\n\n\"Even the kettle has opinions,\" I whispered.\n\n\"It always has.\"\n\nWe spent the rest of the evening together after closing, private and unhurried. The next morning we still had to count the tea stock and write to the growers. The world had not rearranged itself to remove every fear.\n\nBut when Iroh set out the Pai Sho board again, he met my eyes.\n\n\"Perhaps,\" he said, \"I will let you win one day.\"\n\n\"That sounds suspiciously like cheating.\"\n\n\"Then I shall have to win honestly.\"\n\nHe did.","word_count":1363},"avatar-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Empty Chair Is Not a Threat","content":"The chair beside the window had been empty for twelve days.\n\nI knew because Mara had asked whether the teahouse needed another delivery of bread, and Iroh had said, \"Only if Mr. Halen returns.\" He said it lightly, but I noticed his eyes move toward the small table where the old man usually sat.\n\nHalen was not an especially cheerful customer. He came three mornings a week, drank one cup of the house blend, complained about the weather, and left two coins on the table even when the price was lower. He had once informed me that my sweeping technique lacked discipline.\n\nI liked him.\n\nWhen he stopped coming, I assumed he had found another shop.\n\nIroh did not.\n\n\"Would you like to check on him?\" I asked.\n\n\"I would,\" he said. \"But I do not wish to make his absence into an accusation.\"\n\n\"Then we can ask whether he's well. He can tell us to go away.\"\n\nIroh considered that.\n\n\"An excellent outcome, if he is well enough to do so.\"\n\nWe found the address through the delivery notes Halen had voluntarily given the teahouse for winter weather. Iroh checked that the contact permission included welfare visits, not just tea delivery. It did.\n\nHe wrapped a small packet of bread and tea leaves, and we walked up Jasmine Steps toward the narrow houses behind the herb gardens.\n\nThe path was damp. Water ran through the channels between the stones, carrying the faint smell of rosemary. At Halen's gate, a blue flowerpot lay on its side.\n\nIroh knocked.\n\nNothing happened.\n\nHe knocked again, then stepped back.\n\nA window opened above us.\n\n\"Go away,\" Halen called.\n\nI looked at Iroh.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good morning, Mr. Halen. We noticed you have not visited. We wished to know whether you are well.\"\n\n\"I'm alive.\"\n\n\"An encouraging beginning.\"\n\n\"I don't want tea.\"\n\n\"Then we shall not insist.\"\n\nThe window began to close.\n\nI spoke before I could stop myself.\n\n\"Do you need help with the flowerpot?\"\n\nThere was a pause.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"The one by the gate. It's fallen over.\"\n\nHalen looked down.\n\n\"Blasted wind.\"\n\n\"Would you like me to set it upright?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Yes. Don't break it.\"\n\nI picked up the pot and found a crack along the base. The soil had spilled into the path, and the plant's roots were exposed.\n\n\"This needs a new pot,\" I said.\n\nHalen sighed.\n\n\"Of course it does.\"\n\nIroh looked up at him.\n\n\"May we come in with it?\"\n\nA long silence followed.\n\nThen we heard the stairs.\n\nHalen opened the door wearing a coat over his nightclothes. His hair was uncombed. The house behind him smelled of dust and old paper.\n\n\"Five minutes,\" he said.\n\nWe went inside.\n\nThe room was filled with boxes. On the table lay a bundle of letters tied with blue string. Halen noticed me looking and put a hand over them.\n\n\"Private.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" I said.\n\nI turned away.\n\nWe found a spare pot in the kitchen and moved the plant into it. Iroh made tea only after Halen asked for some. The bread stayed wrapped on the counter until he decided whether he wanted it.\n\nFor the first ten minutes we talked about weather and soil.\n\nThen Halen sat down.\n\n\"My sister died,\" he said.\n\nI felt the words more than heard them.\n\nIroh placed his cup on the table.\n\n\"I am sorry.\"\n\nHalen nodded.\n\n\"She used to write to me every week. We argued about everything. The proper way to prune roses. Whether the sea-glass orchard was a waste of good stone. Whether I should stop eating so much bread.\"\n\nI looked at the bundle of letters.\n\nHe followed my glance.\n\n\"I don't want anyone to read them.\"\n\n\"Then nobody will,\" I said.\n\nHalen stared at the table.\n\n\"I stopped coming to the teahouse because she liked it. She always asked whether I had seen the old general who served jasmine tea. I would tell her he was overbearing.\"\n\nIroh smiled faintly.\n\n\"An accurate assessment.\"\n\nHalen's mouth twitched.\n\n\"I didn't want to sit in the chair and think about how I couldn't tell her it was raining.\"\n\nHalen touched the edge of his empty cup.\n\n\"She hated this blend,\" he said. \"Always told me it tasted like somebody had brewed the rain.\"\n\nIroh laughed softly. \"A severe but memorable criticism.\"\n\n\"She liked the sound of this room. The cups. The door. People arriving.\"\n\nI thought of the twelve empty mornings and how easily we'd imagined that his absence was a message to us. It had been a grief we couldn't see.\n\n\"Would you like us to send a note when the shop has a quiet afternoon?\" I asked.\n\nHe considered. \"Perhaps. But don't start checking on me every time I miss tea.\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"You can come when you want.\"\n\nHe looked surprised by the simplicity of it. Iroh set down the kettle and asked whether he might bring Halen another cup now, or whether he'd prefer to be left alone.\n\n\"Another cup,\" Halen said. \"And I still think the tea is weak.\"\n\nIroh smiled in a way that made my chest ache.\n\nWhen Halen had gone, I found Iroh carefully wiping down the table beside the window. I took the cloth from him only after he held it out.\n\n\"You're thinking of Lu Ten,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes. And of how many people here have stories that don't belong to me.\"\n\nWe stood beside each other for a while. I had told him about my polyamorous relationships and the people I loved elsewhere; he had told me how it sometimes hurt to see an empty chair. Neither truth canceled the other.\n\n\"Would you like some company?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked at the table, then at me. \"I would.\"\n\nWe put away the clean cups together. It was almost closing time. Outside the street vendors were calling their final prices, and nothing about the city's ordinary noise had become less important because one person was grieving.\n\nI understood then why the empty chair mattered. Not because a regular customer had stopped spending coins, but because a small ordinary place had become painful to enter.\n\n\"Do you want us to keep the chair for you?\" I asked.\n\nHalen looked startled.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"You're not going to insist?\"\n\n\"No. You can come back if you want. You can choose a different seat. Or you can stay home.\"\n\nIroh nodded.\n\n\"The table will remain part of the teahouse, but you are not a duty it must fulfill.\"\n\nHalen looked at him for a long moment.\n\n\"You're a strange man.\"\n\n\"Often.\"\n\nHe finally unwrapped the bread.\n\nWe left after half an hour, not because we were unwelcome but because Halen had asked for some quiet. He kept the plant on the windowsill and accepted the tea leaves.\n\nOn the walk back, Iroh was silent.\n\n\"Are you thinking about Lu Ten?\" I asked.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Would you like to talk?\"\n\n\"Not today.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nHe looked grateful for the simplicity of the answer.\n\nAt the teahouse, the chair by the window remained empty. We cleaned it like every other chair and set the day's menu on the table. Iroh did not leave a cup waiting. I did not watch the door every few minutes.\n\nA week later, Halen returned.\n\nHe walked in carrying the repaired flowerpot and set it on the counter.\n\n\"Your tea was too strong,\" he told Iroh.\n\n\"Then I shall make it weaker.\"\n\n\"And the bread was too sweet.\"\n\n\"Mara will be devastated.\"\n\nHe glanced toward the window seat.\n\nThen he chose a table near the back.\n\nIroh carried his tea there without comment.\n\nAfter closing, I found him washing the cups alone. I stood beside him and took a towel.\n\n\"Happy?\" I asked.\n\n\"Relieved.\"\n\n\"That he came back?\"\n\n\"That he chose to.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nIroh turned toward me, his hands damp from the washbasin.\n\n\"Thank you for not making his grief into a project.\"\n\n\"I almost did.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said gently. \"So did I.\"\n\nI leaned close enough to feel his breath against my cheek.\n\n\"Do you want company tonight?\"\n\nHe touched the back of my hand.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\nThe kiss was soft and brief, a promise of nothing more than being together when we both wanted it.\n\nThe next morning, the chair by the window was occupied by a new customer who asked whether the house blend contained citrus.\n\nIt did.\n\nHalen sat at the back with his tea, reading a letter he had chosen to bring.\n\nNobody asked who it was from.","word_count":1433},"avatar-50":{"number":50,"title":"Tea for Whoever Returns","content":"The first cup after the repair was terrible.\n\nIroh knew before he tasted it.\n\nMina had set the kettle too close to the new stove vent, and the water heated faster than expected. The leaves steeped too long while everyone admired the fact that the second kitchen was finally dry.\n\nHe took one sip and made a face.\n\nI nearly laughed tea through my nose.\n\n\"Well?\" I asked.\n\n\"Powerful,\" he said.\n\n\"That bad?\"\n\n\"One might mistake it for a declaration of war.\"\n\nMina groaned.\n\nIroh put down the cup.\n\n\"Then let's make another.\"\n\nThe repaired kitchen was smaller than the plans had promised, but safer. The backflow device had been tested under the next storm conditions. Tamsin had signed off on the new drainage channel. Bo had helped revise the cleaning checklist, and the staff had been paid for the extra days spent moving supplies.\n\nA new sign beside the back door explained what the warning sounds meant and whom to contact if the drain began to gurgle.\n\nIt wasn't a beautiful sign.\n\nIt might save the shop.\n\nThe public room was ready too. We had moved the tables to keep a clear path from the entrance to the garden, and Edda had tested the turn around the window seat. The little table Halen sometimes chose was still near the back. The front table stood unreserved.\n\nThat decision had caused a small argument.\n\n\"Surely,\" Mara said, \"you want to keep one seat for your regulars?\"\n\nIroh shook his head.\n\n\"Regulars have favorite places. They do not own them.\"\n\n\"Then what if someone comes back and finds their chair occupied?\"\n\n\"Then we offer another chair.\"\n\nMara considered.\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nI thought of all the ways people tried to turn belonging into a reserved place, a promise that no one else could sit where they had sat before.\n\nThe teahouse had room for people to return without keeping everyone else waiting.\n\nWe opened at noon.\n\nThe first customer was a child with a very muddy boot. The second was Halen, who inspected the new floor and informed us that the old boards had been less slippery.\n\n\"That's because they were cracked,\" I said.\n\n\"Cracks can be useful,\" he replied.\n\nIroh offered him tea.\n\nHe accepted, but chose a different table from his usual one.\n\nA little later, Danse came in with Toph to discuss the shared drainage report. They argued about a measurement near the canal and then, astonishingly, agreed on the next test. Greg arrived carrying a small stack of chairs for the upcoming public meeting. Maui helped bring them inside without announcing his heroic arrival.\n\nI watched the room fill with the people whose work had made the reopening possible.\n\nNot an audience.\n\nA community.\n\nAt the counter, Iroh was pouring the affordable house blend. He had kept its price steady through the repair, with the costs recorded openly in the ledger. The growers were still facing a difficult season, and the clinic still needed a reserved supply of mountain mint.\n\nWe hadn't solved scarcity by wishing.\n\nWe'd changed how we shared it.\n\nIn the late afternoon, a traveler arrived through the Jasmine Door carrying a small bag and looking uncertain. They stood just inside the entrance, watching the room.\n\nIroh approached gently.\n\n\"Welcome. Would you like a table?\"\n\nThe traveler glanced toward the empty one by the window.\n\n\"Is that reserved?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do I need to tell you how long I'm staying?\"\n\n\"Only if you wish to make an arrangement.\"\n\nThey smiled, relieved.\n\n\"I'd like tea.\"\n\n\"Then tea it is.\"\n\nHe seated them without asking for a story.\n\nI watched from behind the counter, feeling something settle inside me.\n\nA second customer came in just before closing, carrying a small parcel from a neighboring stall. The paper had been wrapped in string and tied so badly that the whole package looked ready to fall apart.\n\n\"I heard the kitchen reopened,\" they said. \"My mother wanted you to have this.\"\n\nInside was a little clay cup, mismatched with every cup in the shop. Iroh held it carefully.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because she used to stop here after market day. She said you gave her tea when she was tired.\"\n\nHe looked touched, then uncomfortable. \"We can't accept a gift every time we sell a cup.\"\n\n\"You don't have to display it. She just wanted you to know she remembered.\"\n\nWe thanked them. After they left, Iroh put the clay cup on the shelf with the shop's other mismatched pieces rather than giving it a place of honor that would make the customer feel indebted next time.\n\n\"The valley taught me a great many things,\" he said. \"I am trying to bring the useful ones home.\"\n\n\"This is already your home,\" I told him.\n\nHe smiled. The Jasmine Dragon was a real Ba Sing Se business, with workers who needed wages, growers who deserved fair prices and customers whose kindness didn't erase a debt. The Lanternweald had its own Jasmine Steps, and its own stories. I loved visiting both without confusing them.\n\nIroh took my hand across the account book. \"Would you like to stay a while?\"\n\n\"Yes. I have other plans later in the week, and I know you do too. Tonight I'd like to be here.\"\n\nHe nodded. We had learned to speak about ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory in ordinary sentences, not just at moments when one of us feared being left behind.\n\n\"Then let us finish the accounts,\" he said, \"and make the next cup properly.\"\n\nI laughed. The shop needed no grand ending to stay alive after a story finished. It needed a kettle, people who chose to come in, and people willing to open the door again tomorrow.\n\nAfter closing, we counted the till. It wasn't an impressive sum. The repair debt remained, and the next delivery would still cost more than we'd hoped.\n\nIroh checked the figures and closed the ledger.\n\n\"Enough for the next step,\" he said.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You're learning to sound like Greg.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I shall acquire a guitar.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe carried the cups into the second kitchen. The walls smelled faintly of fresh plaster, and the window had been repaired so it no longer rattled when the wind changed.\n\nIroh set a kettle on the stove.\n\n\"Would you like tea?\" he asked.\n\n\"Only if you're making it.\"\n\n\"An alarming amount of trust.\"\n\n\"You've earned it.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe tea was good this time, warm and gently bitter, with the citrus at the end just as it should be.\n\nWe drank in silence for a while.\n\nThen he took my hand.\n\n\"Are you happy?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even when the roof leaks and the drain floods and your life is full of complicated people?\"\n\n\"Especially then, sometimes. Not because the problems are good. Because we can face them honestly.\"\n\nHe looked thoughtful.\n\n\"I once believed peace was a place one reached after every battle had ended.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think it is sometimes a cup of tea while tomorrow remains uncertain.\"\n\nI set down my cup and moved closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I was hoping you would.\"\n\nHis lips met mine, warm and patient. He held my hand against his chest, and I felt the quiet rhythm of his heartbeat. We kissed again, slower this time, with no need to make the moment a declaration against any of my other relationships.\n\nI loved him. That was enough.\n\nLater we went upstairs, leaving the last clean cups to dry. The evening stayed private, full of soft laughter and the ordinary comfort of being wanted without being owned.\n\nAt dawn, I woke to the smell of tea.\n\nIroh stood by the window with two cups and a look of tremendous satisfaction.\n\n\"You're up early,\" I said.\n\n\"The kettle is punctual.\"\n\n\"Unlike the roof.\"\n\n\"An unfortunate comparison.\"\n\nI took a cup.\n\nOutside, the streets of Ba Sing Se glittered with rainwater that was flowing the proper direction. People were opening shops, sweeping paths, carrying bread, and deciding where to go next.\n\nThe table by the window remained unreserved.\n\nWe would make tea for whoever chose to return.\n\nAnd when the day was done, we would still be allowed to choose one another.","word_count":1377}}};for(const s of lib.series){const e=revisions[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const r=e[c.id];if(!r||c.chapter_number!==r.number)continue;c.content=r.content;c.word_count=r.word_count;c.title=r.title;}}})();
